QB.push(
{id:1081,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A streaming client handles tool use like this: for each `content_block_delta` whose delta type is `input_json_delta`, it immediately calls `JSON.parse(delta.partial_json)`. It worked in testing against a one-argument tool; in production with multi-argument tools it throws `Unexpected end of JSON input` on most calls. What is wrong?",
o:["A. `input_json_delta` carries a base64 payload that must be decoded before parsing","B. The client should parse `delta.input` rather than `delta.partial_json`","C. `partial_json` fragments are pieces of one JSON string and are only valid JSON once concatenated across the whole block","D. Tool arguments only stream correctly when `disable_parallel_tool_use` is set"],a:2,
e:"The deltas for a `tool_use` block are **partial JSON strings**, split at arbitrary character boundaries — `{\"location\":` then ` \"San Fra`. No individual fragment need be valid JSON. Accumulate them into a string builder keyed by the block's `index`, then parse once, when `content_block_stop` arrives for that index. The final `tool_use.input` in the assembled message is always an object. Transferable principle: streamed structures are fragments of a value, not a sequence of complete values.",
w:"A — no base64 encoding is involved; `partial_json` is plain text. B — the delta on an `input_json_delta` has no `input` field; the assembled object appears as `tool_use.input` on the final message. D — parallel tool use governs how many tool calls one turn may contain, not the delta encoding."},

{id:1082,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A billing service tallies output tokens for streamed responses by adding `usage.output_tokens` from every `message_delta` event it receives. Reported output volume runs roughly double the invoice on long responses. What is the defect?",
o:["A. `message_delta` usage counts are cumulative, so the final event already holds the total and earlier values must not be summed","B. `message_delta` reports usage for the whole conversation, so prior turns are being counted again","C. Output tokens must be read from `message_stop`, which carries the authoritative total","D. Streamed output is billed at half rate, so the raw counts need halving"],a:0,
e:"The docs warn explicitly that the token counts in a `message_delta` event's `usage` field are **cumulative**. A stream may emit more than one `message_delta`, so summing them double-counts everything reported before the last one. Take the value from the final `message_delta` and treat earlier ones as running totals. Input-side counters (`input_tokens`, and the cache fields when caching is in play) arrive separately, on `message_start`.",
w:"B — the counts cover the current response only, not accumulated history. C — `message_stop` carries nothing but its `type`; there is no usage on it. D — streaming carries no discount at all; the Batch API is what halves price."},

{id:1083,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which set lists delta types that can appear inside a `content_block_delta` event?",
o:["A. `text_delta`, `json_delta`, `reasoning_delta`, `usage_delta`","B. `text_delta`, `tool_delta`, `thinking_delta`, `stop_delta`","C. `content_delta`, `input_json_delta`, `signature_delta`, `ping_delta`","D. `text_delta`, `input_json_delta`, `thinking_delta`, `signature_delta`"],a:3,
e:"Four delta types are documented: **`text_delta`** for prose, **`input_json_delta`** carrying `partial_json` for tool inputs, **`thinking_delta`** for reasoning text, and **`signature_delta`**, emitted once just before `content_block_stop` on a thinking block to verify that block's integrity. Version-sensitivity note: the streaming events and delta types are governed by the versioning policy and the set may grow, so a parser needs a default branch rather than an exhaustive switch.",
w:"A — there is no `json_delta` or `usage_delta`; usage rides on `message_start` and `message_delta`. B — tool arguments stream as `input_json_delta`, and stopping is signalled by the `content_block_stop` event, not a `stop_delta`. C — `ping` is a top-level event rather than a delta, and no `content_delta` exists."},

{id:1084,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A service streams a long response. Ten seconds in, it receives `event: error` with `{\"type\":\"error\",\"error\":{\"type\":\"overloaded_error\",\"message\":\"Overloaded\"}}`. The team's HTTP retry wrapper, which retries 429 and 5xx, never fires. Why, and what is needed?",
o:["A. The wrapper is correct; an `overloaded_error` inside a stream is informational and the stream still completes","B. The HTTP response already returned 200, so mid-stream errors bypass status-code retry logic entirely and must be handled inside the event loop","C. Error events are only emitted when streaming is combined with tools, so the wrapper needs a tool-specific branch","D. The wrapper should retry on HTTP 529, which the stream sets retroactively in a trailer"],a:1,
e:"Once headers are sent the request is a **200**, so an error arriving later does not follow the normal mechanisms — including the SDKs' automatic backoff. The docs note this directly: an error can occur after the API returns a 200 response, and in that case error handling does not follow the standard path. This `overloaded_error` would have been a 529 non-streaming. The client must detect the `error` event itself, decide whether to keep the partial output, and re-issue with backoff. Streaming gives you two error channels, not one.",
w:"A — an error event ends useful output; treating it as informational ships a silently truncated answer. C — error events can arrive on any stream, with or without tools. D — the HTTP status is fixed once headers are sent; there is no retroactive status and no trailer."},

{id:1085,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A hand-rolled SSE parser dispatches on event name with an exhaustive switch and throws on anything else. It runs fine for months, then begins failing mid-conversation for a subset of requests after a platform update. What principle was violated?",
o:["A. SSE event names are case-sensitive and the platform switched to uppercase names","B. Events must be subscribed to explicitly in the request body, which the update made mandatory","C. The versioning policy permits new event types to be added, so clients need a default branch that ignores events they do not recognise","D. `ping` events were introduced by the update, and they are the only event a parser may safely skip"],a:2,
e:"The streaming docs state that **new event types may be added and your code should handle unknown event types gracefully** — the same additive-change contract that requires a default branch for unrecognised `stop_reason` values. Forward compatibility means ignoring what you do not understand rather than treating it as corruption. A throwing default turns a routine, backwards-compatible platform change into an outage.",
w:"A — event names have not changed case, and case sensitivity is not the failure mode described. B — there is no event-subscription mechanism; the server decides which events to emit. D — `ping` is long-standing, and it is not the only event that can safely be skipped."},

{id:1086,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A streaming UI keeps one string buffer and appends `delta.text` whenever a `content_block_delta` arrives. When a response includes a `tool_use` block, raw fragments such as `{\"location\": \"San Fra` appear inside the chat bubble. What is the correct fix?",
o:["A. Filter the stream so only `content_block_delta` events with an even `index` reach the UI","B. Record the block type from each `content_block_start` and route later deltas for that `index` by type, rendering only text blocks","C. Set `disable_parallel_tool_use` so tool blocks are not streamed","D. Read the finished response from `message_stop` instead of rendering incrementally"],a:1,
e:"**`content_block_start` is the schema for everything that follows on that `index`** — it carries `content_block.type` (`text`, `tool_use`, `thinking`, `server_tool_use`, …) plus, for a tool, its `id` and `name`. Keep an index-to-type map and dispatch: `text_delta` to prose, `input_json_delta` to a per-index argument accumulator, `thinking_delta` to a reasoning pane. Appending `delta.text` blindly also silently drops nothing — `input_json_delta` has no `text` field, so what leaks depends on how loosely the client reads the delta.",
w:"A — index parity carries no meaning; block order and count vary per response. C — that option limits how many tool calls a turn may contain and does not stop tool blocks from streaming. D — `message_stop` carries no content, and abandoning incremental rendering discards the whole point of streaming."},

{id:1087,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What does the `message_start` event of a streamed response contain?",
o:["A. A `Message` object with an empty `content` array, carrying the message `id`, `model`, `role` and initial `usage`","B. The first `text_delta` of the response, so rendering can begin immediately","C. The complete `Message` with `content` populated, which later deltas then revise","D. Only an event `type`; all metadata arrives in `message_delta`"],a:0,
e:"`message_start` opens the stream with the **envelope**: `id`, `role`, `model`, an empty `content` array, `stop_reason: null`, and a `usage` object carrying `input_tokens` — plus `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens` when prompt caching is in play. Content arrives afterwards, block by block. Practically, this means you can log the message id and the input-side cost before a single token of prose exists.",
w:"B — the first text arrives in a `content_block_delta`, after a `content_block_start`. C — `content` is empty at this point; blocks are constructed by the delta events that follow. D — `message_stop` is the event that carries nothing but its `type`."},

{id:1088,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A chat UI has a Stop button. Its handler sets a flag so the reader loop stops appending text to the view, but leaves the loop running to drain the response. Under load the service exhausts its outbound connection pool. What should Stop do instead?",
o:["A. Send a second request with `stream: false` to supersede the first","B. Set `max_tokens: 1` on the in-flight request so the model stops early","C. Keep draining but discard events after the flag is set, because an SSE stream cannot be interrupted by the client","D. Abort the underlying HTTP request — exit the SDK's stream context manager, or fire an abort signal — which closes the connection and releases it"],a:3,
e:"Streaming holds **one HTTP connection open for the whole generation**, so cancellation is a transport concern: close the connection. The SDKs expose exactly this through stream context managers and abort signals. Ignoring events while still holding the socket keeps paying the scarce resource — connections and worker slots — for output nobody will read. Note the honest limit: closing stops delivery and frees resources; it does not rewind tokens the model has already produced.",
w:"A — requests are independent, and a new call cannot cancel an earlier one. B — parameters are fixed when the request is sent and cannot be altered mid-flight. C — the client can always close the connection; SSE is ordinary HTTP, not a protocol that forbids hang-up."},

{id:1089,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A cost guard must record the input-token cost of a streamed request as early as possible, so that unexpectedly expensive prompts can be flagged before the response finishes. Which event gives it that information first?",
o:["A. `message_start`, whose `usage` reports `input_tokens` together with `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens`","B. `content_block_start` for index 0, the first event that reflects consumed input","C. `message_delta`, the only event that carries a `usage` object","D. `ping`, which the API emits once input accounting has settled"],a:0,
e:"Input accounting is **settled before generation begins**, so `message_start` opens the stream with the input-side counters, including the cache fields when a `cache_control` breakpoint is in use. `message_delta` later carries cumulative output usage plus the final `stop_reason`. A guard that waits for the end of the stream to learn what the prompt cost has already spent the money and lost the chance to intervene.",
w:"B — `content_block_start` describes the block about to stream and reports no token counts. C — `message_delta` does carry usage, but it arrives at the end of the stream, which defeats an early guard. D — `ping` is a keep-alive carrying only its `type`."},

{id:1090,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Instrumentation of a streamed tool-calling response shows a 900 ms gap with no events, then several `input_json_delta` events in quick succession, then another gap. The text portion of the same response streamed smoothly, token by token. What explains the pattern?",
o:["A. The gaps are network retransmissions; enabling TCP keep-alive removes them","B. Tool-argument deltas are throttled separately from text deltas by the output-tokens-per-minute limit","C. Current models emit one complete key and value of the tool input at a time, which is then chunked into several `partial_json` deltas — so tool blocks arrive in bursts, and fine-grained tool streaming exists to reduce that buffering","D. `input_json_delta` events are withheld until the accumulated arguments validate against the tool's `input_schema`"],a:2,
e:"The docs note that current models **only support emitting one complete key and value property from `input` at a time**, so there may be delays between streaming events while the model works; once a pair is complete it is emitted as multiple chunked `partial_json` deltas. Burst-then-pause is therefore expected, not pathological. **Fine-grained tool streaming** trades that buffering for latency. Design rule: never treat inter-event silence in a stream as failure — that is what `ping` and an idle-reset timeout are for.",
w:"A — the gaps are server-side generation behaviour, not packet loss; keep-alive addresses idle-connection teardown. B — rate limits constrain requests and tokens per minute overall; they do not shape delta timing by block type. D — the API does not withhold deltas pending schema validation; strict tool use constrains generation instead."},

{id:1091,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which setting enables fine-grained tool streaming, so that tool input JSON is emitted without server-side buffering?",
o:["A. `tool_choice: {\"type\": \"any\"}` on the request","B. `eager_input_streaming` on the individual tool definition","C. `stream_tool_input: true` alongside `stream: true`","D. `disable_parallel_tool_use: false` on the request"],a:1,
e:"Fine-grained tool streaming is opted into **per tool**, via **`eager_input_streaming`** on that tool's definition. The trade is latency against well-formedness: fragments can arrive before the model has finished a complete key and value, so the accumulator must tolerate a buffer that is not parseable until `content_block_stop`. Default behaviour buffers each complete property server-side, which is why unmodified tool streams arrive in bursts.",
w:"A — `tool_choice` decides whether a tool must be used, not how its input streams. C — no such parameter exists. D — parallel tool use governs how many tool calls one turn may contain."},

{id:1092,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"A nightly enrichment job calls Claude 40,000 times, each response about 200 tokens, written straight into a database. No human watches output as it is produced. A developer proposes adding SSE streaming \"for performance\". What is the sound judgement?",
o:["A. Stream, because streaming reduces total generation time per request","B. Stream, because streamed responses are billed at a lower rate","C. Stream, because only streaming reports per-request `usage`","D. Do not stream: with no incremental consumer, streaming adds event handling and a long-lived connection per request without improving cost or total latency — and this workload is a Batch API candidate"],a:3,
e:"Streaming buys **perceived** latency — time to first token — for a consumer that renders progressively. A database writer consumes the whole response at once, so per-event handling adds a parser, partial-state handling and a held connection for zero benefit. The real lever for deadline-free bulk work is the **Batch API**: roughly a 50% discount, a 24-hour window, results as one JSONL file. Choose transport by who is waiting.",
w:"A — total generation time is essentially unchanged; only delivery is incremental. B — streaming carries no discount whatsoever. C — non-streaming responses return a `usage` object too."},

{id:1093,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A report generator sets `max_tokens: 120000` on a non-streaming Messages call. The official SDK raises a validation error before any HTTP request leaves the process, mentioning a timeout. What is happening, and what are the supported options?",
o:["A. `max_tokens: 120000` exceeds the API maximum and would return 400; lower it below the model ceiling","B. The SDK is enforcing an output-tokens-per-minute rate limit locally; request a higher usage tier","C. The SDKs validate that a non-streaming Messages request is not expected to exceed a 10-minute timeout; use the streaming Messages API, or the Message Batches API, for generations this long","D. Long generations require `output_config.effort: \"max\"`, without which the SDK refuses the call"],a:2,
e:"The docs state that the SDKs **validate that your non-streaming Messages API requests are not expected to exceed a 10-minute timeout**, and also set TCP keep-alive. The guidance for anything likely to run past ten minutes is to stream or to batch, because networks drop idle connections and a request can fail with no response at all. If you do not want incremental events, stream anyway and let the SDK accumulate the final `Message`.",
w:"A — this guard fires client-side before any request; a ceiling breach would instead be a server-side 400. B — rate limits are enforced by the API with 429s, not pre-flighted by the SDK. D — `output_config.effort` steers reasoning depth and has no bearing on this check."},

{id:1094,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A long-running non-streaming request returns HTTP `504` with error type `timeout_error`. What is the documented remedy?",
o:["A. Use the streaming Messages API, or the Message Batches API, for long-running requests","B. Retry immediately; `timeout_error` is always transient and succeeds on a second attempt","C. Lower `max_tokens` until the response fits inside the output-tokens-per-minute limit","D. Add an `anthropic-beta` header that extends the request timeout"],a:0,
e:"**`504 timeout_error`** means the request timed out while processing, and the docs point straight at streaming or batching for long-running work. Streaming keeps the connection productive and lets you consume partial output; batching removes the requirement for an uninterrupted connection entirely, since you poll for results. Both also sidestep the idle-connection teardown that makes very long single requests fragile.",
w:"B — an identical long generation reproduces the timeout and doubles the spend. C — output rate limits produce 429s rather than 504s, and `max_tokens` does not factor into output-token rate limits at all. D — no beta header extends the request timeout."},

{id:1095,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A worker needs the complete `Message` object, identical to what a non-streaming call returns, but its generations are long enough to risk an idle-connection timeout. It has no use for events one at a time. What is the cleanest approach?",
o:["A. Call with `stream: false` and raise the HTTP client timeout to 60 minutes","B. Open a stream and use the SDK's accumulation helper — `get_final_message()` in Python, `finalMessage()` in TypeScript, `Accumulate` in Go — to return the assembled `Message`","C. Stream, append every `text_delta` to a string, and treat that string as the response","D. Submit the request twice, streamed and non-streamed, and use whichever returns first"],a:1,
e:"Every SDK exposes this pattern: stream underneath for connection resilience, hand back the same `Message` object `.create()` would have returned. Python `get_final_message()`, TypeScript `finalMessage()`, Go `message.Accumulate(event)`, Java `MessageAccumulator`, Ruby `.accumulated_message`, C# `.Aggregate()`. You get the timeout protection without writing an event loop, and you keep everything a text-only accumulation would have thrown away.",
w:"A — a longer client timeout does not stop intermediate networks dropping an idle connection, and the SDKs guard non-streaming calls at ten minutes anyway. C — a concatenated string discards `tool_use` and `thinking` blocks, `stop_reason` and `usage`. D — duplicating the request doubles cost and can yield two different answers."},

{id:1096,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A stream on a current model dies at roughly 70% of a long answer. The team's recovery code appends the received text as a trailing `assistant` message and re-sends, expecting continuation. Every retry now returns `400 invalid_request_error`. What is the current pattern?",
o:["A. Re-send with `stream: false` so the response arrives in one piece and no continuation is needed","B. Re-send the identical request; interrupted streams are replayed from a server-side cache","C. Append the partial text as a `tool_result` block so the model treats it as prior work","D. Send the captured partial inside a `user` message that instructs the model to continue from where it left off"],a:3,
e:"Capture-and-resume still works, but step two changed. Claude 4.6 and later **reject assistant-message prefill** — \"This model does not support assistant message prefill. The conversation must end with a user message.\" The documented replacement is a `user` message carrying the partial, for example: \"Your previous response was interrupted and ended with [previous_response]. Continue from where you left off.\" **Currency note:** the assistant-prefill continuation trick is legacy and now returns 400.",
w:"A — a non-streaming re-run regenerates from scratch, pays for the discarded work again, and risks the ten-minute timeout. B — the API is stateless; there is no server-side replay of an interrupted stream. C — a `tool_result` block must match a real `tool_use_id`, and an unmatched id is itself a 400."},

{id:1097,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A resume-after-interruption helper must decide which received content it can reuse. The dead stream had delivered a complete `thinking` block, a partial `tool_use` block, and a partial `text` block. What does Anthropic's guidance permit resuming from?",
o:["A. Any block, since accumulated `partial_json` can be repaired by a partial-JSON parser","B. Only the `thinking` block, because its `signature_delta` proves integrity","C. The most recent text block; `tool_use` and extended thinking blocks cannot be partially recovered","D. Nothing; recovery means restarting the request from the original prompt"],a:2,
e:"The error-recovery guidance is explicit: messages can contain multiple content block types, **tool use and extended thinking blocks cannot be partially recovered**, and you can resume streaming from the most recent text block. Half a tool call is unusable — you cannot execute it safely and you cannot replay it as history. And a truncated thinking block cannot be replayed either, because thinking blocks must be returned exactly as received or the request 400s.",
w:"A — a syntactically repaired tool input is a guess at the model's intent and may execute the wrong action with real side effects. B — a truncated thinking block is not replayable; integrity verification is precisely why it cannot be reconstructed. D — a full restart wastes intact leading text that is reusable."},

{id:1098,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A client streams a response with thinking enabled and stores only the concatenated `thinking_delta` text, discarding the `signature_delta` that arrives just before `content_block_stop`. On the next turn, replaying that thinking block returns `400 invalid_request_error`. Why does the signature matter?",
o:["A. The signature verifies the integrity of the thinking block, and thinking blocks must be replayed exactly as received — edited, filtered or reconstructed blocks are rejected","B. The signature is the decryption key for the thinking text, without which the API cannot read it","C. The signature encodes the thinking token count, which the API needs for billing on the following turn","D. The signature is only required when thinking is combined with tool use"],a:0,
e:"**`signature_delta`** is emitted once per thinking block, immediately before `content_block_stop`, and it verifies that block's integrity. The docs state that `thinking` or `redacted_thinking` blocks in the latest assistant message which were edited, reordered, filtered out or reconstructed produce a 400 naming the offending position, for example `messages.1.content.0`. Rule: capture thinking blocks whole — text plus signature — and pass them back untouched, including blocks whose `thinking` field is empty.",
w:"B — the thinking text arrives in clear `thinking_delta` events; the signature is not a key. C — token accounting lives in `usage`, not in the signature. D — the constraint applies whenever a thinking block is replayed, with or without tools."},

{id:1099,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A React chat view re-renders a full markdown tree on every `text_delta`. During fast responses the browser drops frames and typing in the input box stutters. Which change addresses the cause without giving up progressive display?",
o:["A. Request a larger delta granularity from the API so fewer events arrive","B. Buffer incoming deltas and flush the accumulated text into state once per animation frame","C. Stop rendering until `message_stop`, then render once","D. Move the fetch into a Web Worker so deltas arrive on a background thread"],a:1,
e:"Delta size is a server-side detail: `text_delta` events are deliberately fine-grained and can arrive faster than a browser can lay out a markdown tree. **Decouple arrival rate from render rate** — accumulate into a buffer and commit on a `requestAnimationFrame` tick, so text still appears continuous at a rate the compositor sustains. Transferable principle: a stream gives you an event rate, not a render schedule.",
w:"A — there is no client-controlled delta granularity, and the one related knob, fine-grained tool streaming, makes tool deltas finer rather than coarser. C — waiting for the end abandons progressive display, the reason for streaming. D — a worker moves the network read, but state commits still land on the main thread at the same rate."},

{id:1100,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A streamed endpoint works from a developer laptop. Deployed behind the company's reverse proxy, clients see nothing for 25 seconds and then the entire answer at once. Connections are never reset and no events are lost. What is the most likely cause?",
o:["A. `ping` events are being stripped, so the browser withholds data until the stream closes","B. The proxy is downgrading to HTTP/1.0, which cannot carry SSE","C. The API is buffering internally because `max_tokens` is set too high","D. The proxy is buffering the response body and flushing only when the upstream connection closes; response buffering must be disabled for the streaming route"],a:3,
e:"Complete, correctly ordered, late output is the signature of **response buffering**, not of a transport failure. Proxies and gateways buffer by default to shield origins from slow clients; for SSE that must be turned off per route so each event flushes as produced. Contrast the two proxy failure modes: buffering loses only timing, whereas an idle-timeout teardown loses the tail and resets the connection. Diagnose by curling the origin directly, bypassing the proxy.",
w:"A — stripping keep-alives risks idle-timeout teardown, but it cannot make a proxy hold bytes it has already received. B — SSE runs fine over HTTP/1.1 chunked transfer, and a genuine protocol downgrade would break the request rather than delay it intact. C — `max_tokens` is an output cap; the API emits deltas as they are generated regardless of its value."},

{id:1101,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which two events can appear at any point in a Claude SSE stream, rather than at a fixed position in the message lifecycle?",
o:["A. `message_delta` and `message_stop`","B. `content_block_start` and `content_block_stop`","C. `ping` and `error`","D. `usage_update` and `heartbeat`"],a:2,
e:"The lifecycle itself is fixed: `message_start`, then per-block groups of `content_block_start` / `content_block_delta` / `content_block_stop`, then one or more `message_delta`, then `message_stop`. Interleaved with it, **any number of `ping` keep-alives** and, if something fails after the 200, an **`error`** event. A parser must therefore tolerate both at arbitrary positions and must never treat a `ping` as content or as an index.",
w:"A — both sit at defined positions: `message_delta` after the content blocks, `message_stop` last. B — these bracket one specific content block, in order, and each carries an `index`. D — neither event exists in the Messages API stream."},

{id:1102,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A moderation-sensitive product must never display refused content. The team plans to check for `stop_reason: \"refusal\"` and suppress the answer. In a streaming implementation, what problem must the design confront?",
o:["A. `stop_reason` is never emitted on streamed responses, so refusals cannot be detected while streaming","B. `stop_reason` arrives in `message_delta` near the end of the stream, so any text already streamed has reached the user before the refusal is known","C. Refusals must be detected by string-matching the streamed text, since `refusal` is not a `stop_reason` value","D. A refusal aborts the stream before `message_start`, so no events arrive at all"],a:1,
e:"**`refusal`** is one of the seven documented `stop_reason` values, and in a stream `stop_reason` lands on `message_delta`, after the content blocks are finished. Render-as-you-go therefore means you may have to retract text the user already read. The design options are real ones: buffer to a checkpoint before display, accept visible retraction, or do not stream this surface. Note also that `stop_details` is populated only when the stop reason is `refusal`, carrying an open-set `category`.",
w:"A — streamed responses do carry `stop_reason`; it rides on `message_delta`. C — string matching is explicitly the wrong approach, and `refusal` is a first-class `stop_reason`. D — a refusal is a normal, fully-formed response with the usual event lifecycle."},

{id:1103,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which statement about combining `stream: true` with prompt caching is correct?",
o:["A. Streaming changes only how the response is delivered; cache lookup, the tools then system then messages prefix order, and the write and read multipliers are all unchanged","B. Cached prefixes cannot be used with streaming, because a cached prefix would have to be replayed as deltas","C. Streaming forces the cache TTL to the 1-hour option","D. Streaming halves the cache write cost, because output is delivered incrementally"],a:0,
e:"Caching is a property of the **request prefix**; streaming is a property of **response transport**. They are orthogonal, so a streamed request hits or writes the cache exactly as its non-streaming twin would, and the stream reports `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens` in `usage`. All the caching rules still hold: breakpoint on the block ending the cacheable prefix, at most four breakpoints, byte-identical prefix, write at 1.25× base input and read at 0.1×.",
w:"B — the two features compose freely, and cached input is never re-emitted as output deltas. C — TTL comes from the `cache_control` you send, defaulting to five minutes with a 1-hour option. D — streaming has no pricing effect of any kind."},

{id:1104,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A developer building a direct, non-SDK integration sets `content-type: text/event-stream` on a request with `\"stream\": true` and receives `400 invalid_request_error`. What are the correct request headers?",
o:["A. `content-type: text/event-stream`, plus `accept: application/json`","B. No `content-type` is needed once `stream` is set, because the body is then interpreted as SSE","C. `content-type: application/json`, and `anthropic-version` may be omitted when streaming","D. `content-type: application/json` and `anthropic-version`, exactly as for a non-streaming call — the request body is still JSON, and only the response is server-sent events"],a:3,
e:"`stream: true` is a **field in the JSON request body**, so a streamed call is an ordinary JSON POST to `/v1/messages` with `content-type: application/json`, `anthropic-version`, and a credential header (`Authorization: Bearer …` or the legacy `x-api-key`). Streaming changes the representation of the response, not of the request. Confusing the two directions is a classic first-integration failure.",
w:"A — `text/event-stream` describes what the server produces; declaring your JSON body that way is invalid. B — the body is JSON regardless of `stream`, and `content-type` is required. C — `anthropic-version` is required on every request, streaming included."},

{id:1105,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent runtime tries to shave latency: when it sees `content_block_start` with `content_block.type` of `tool_use`, it immediately dispatches the named tool. Roughly one call in twenty runs with missing or wrong arguments. What is the defect?",
o:["A. `content_block_start` does not name the tool, so the runtime is dispatching by guesswork","B. Tool dispatch must wait for `message_stop`, the only point at which tool calls are final","C. The start event announces the block with its `id`, `name` and an empty `input`; the arguments exist only once the `input_json_delta` fragments for that `index` are complete at `content_block_stop`","D. The runtime needs `disable_parallel_tool_use` so that only one `tool_use` block can be in flight"],a:2,
e:"The start event is a genuinely useful early signal — show a spinner, warm a connection, resolve credentials — but it carries `input: {}`. Arguments accumulate as `partial_json` across the deltas for that `index` and are parseable only at that block's `content_block_stop`. **Use the start event for preparation and the stop event for authorisation and execution.** The model proposes; deterministic code executes only complete, validated input.",
w:"A — `content_block_start` does carry the tool `id` and `name`; only `input` is empty. B — waiting for `message_stop` is needlessly late, since a block is final at its own `content_block_stop`. D — parallel tool use governs how many calls a turn may contain, not whether arguments are complete."},

{id:1106,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A latency dashboard reports time-to-first-token for streamed responses. Values look implausibly low and flat, around 40 ms, even for requests with a 150,000-token prompt. Which event should the timer stop on?",
o:["A. The first `content_block_delta`, which carries the first fragment of generated content","B. `message_start`, emitted as soon as the request is accepted","C. The first `ping`, which the API emits when generation begins","D. `content_block_start`, which opens the first content block"],a:0,
e:"`message_start` is the envelope and arrives before the model has produced anything, so timing to it measures request acceptance — which is exactly why 40 ms looks the same for a tiny prompt and a 150,000-token one. `content_block_start` then opens a block that is still empty. The **first `content_block_delta`** is the first observable token; for a thinking-first response that will be a `thinking_delta`. Measure the number a user actually feels.",
w:"B — `message_start` precedes generation entirely, which explains the flat 40 ms. C — `ping` is an arbitrary keep-alive with no relationship to generation progress. D — `content_block_start` announces an empty block; nothing has streamed yet."},

{id:1107,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team streams from Claude inside a serverless function, accumulates the text, and returns it as a JSON body to the browser. Users see no progressive output. The function's own logs show deltas arriving smoothly. What is the correct diagnosis?",
o:["A. SSE cannot cross a serverless boundary; the browser must call the Claude API directly","B. Streaming has to be carried end to end — function response, gateway and CDN must each forward events unbuffered — so the last buffering hop determines what the user sees","C. The browser needs an `EventSource` rather than `fetch`, which cannot read a streamed body","D. The function should request `stream: false` from Claude and stream to the browser instead"],a:1,
e:"Streaming is a property of a whole delivery chain, not of one call. Accumulating upstream events and returning a single JSON body converts the feature back into a blocking request, no matter how well the upstream leg works. Fix it hop by hop: emit a streaming response from the function, choose a runtime and gateway mode that supports response streaming, and disable buffering at any proxy or CDN between. Security corollary: never \"solve\" this by moving the call to the browser — the API key would ship with it.",
w:"A — serverless platforms can stream responses, and putting the key in the browser exposes it to every user. C — `fetch` can read a streamed body through its response stream reader; the client API is not the constraint. D — you cannot stream to the browser what you asked the API to deliver in one piece."},

{id:1108,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"In a streamed `tool_use` block, what is the relationship between the `partial_json` fragments and the tool input the model produced?",
o:["A. Each `partial_json` value is a self-contained JSON object holding one argument","B. The fragments are base64 chunks of a compressed input object","C. The API has already merged them; the last fragment contains the complete input","D. The fragments are pieces of one JSON string; concatenated in arrival order for that `index` they form the input, whose final form in the message is an object"],a:3,
e:"The docs put it precisely: the deltas are **partial JSON strings**, whereas the final `tool_use.input` is always an **object**. So the accumulator is a string builder keyed by `index`, and the parse happens once, at `content_block_stop`. Fragments break at arbitrary character boundaries — `{\"location\":` then ` \"San Fra` then ` CA\"}` — so no single fragment need be valid JSON on its own.",
w:"A — fragments split mid-token and are frequently not valid JSON alone. B — no compression or base64 encoding is applied to tool input deltas. C — the API sends fragments precisely so clients can consume them early; assembling them is the client's job."},

{id:1109,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A chat service streams a response containing a text block and a `tool_use` block, then must issue the next request. What does it have to reconstruct from the stream, and why?",
o:["A. Only the final text, because the API retains the tool call server-side against the message `id`","B. Only the `tool_use` block, because text already displayed need not be replayed","C. The full ordered `content` array — every block, in `index` order, with its type and assembled payload — because the API is stateless and the next request must carry the whole accumulated history","D. Nothing; passing the previous message `id` in `metadata` restores the turn"],a:2,
e:"Streaming is a delivery mechanism, not a session. **The API is stateless**, so the client is the store of record: accumulate per `index` into the same `content` array a non-streaming call would have returned, preserving block order and types, then append matching `tool_result` blocks in a following `user` message. Dropping a `tool_use` block while sending its `tool_result` — or the reverse — yields an unmatched `tool_use_id` and a 400.",
w:"A — nothing is retained server-side; a message `id` is a log handle, not a session key. B — the assistant's text is part of the conversation the model must see next turn. D — `metadata` carries opaque user identifiers and cannot restore state."},

{id:1110,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A product manager asks for a determinate progress bar — \"37% generated\" — during streamed responses. What should the engineer explain?",
o:["A. Divide the accumulated `output_tokens` from `message_delta` by `max_tokens` for an exact percentage","B. No total-length signal exists, because the model does not know in advance how long the answer will be; an indeterminate indicator, or coarse phase markers taken from block boundaries, is the honest option","C. `content_block_start` includes an `expected_length` field for exactly this purpose","D. Setting `output_config.effort` to `low` makes response length deterministic and therefore measurable"],a:1,
e:"A stream reports what has happened, never what remains. `max_tokens` is a ceiling most responses never approach, so a ratio against it produces a bar that crawls then jumps to done. Honest alternatives: an indeterminate spinner, elapsed time, or phase labels derived from the event lifecycle — switch from \"Thinking\" to \"Writing\" when a thinking block stops and a text block starts.",
w:"A — `max_tokens` is an upper bound, not a prediction, so the ratio is not progress. C — no such field exists on any stream event. D — `effort` steers how much reasoning the model does; it does not fix output length."},

{id:1111,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A request enables thinking with `display: \"omitted\"` and streams. What does the client observe for the thinking block?",
o:["A. The block opens with `content_block_start`, receives a single `signature_delta`, and closes with `content_block_stop`; no `thinking_delta` events are sent","B. No thinking block appears in the stream at all","C. `thinking_delta` events still arrive, with the `thinking` field set to an empty string","D. The full thinking content arrives in one `thinking_delta` at `content_block_stop`"],a:0,
e:"Omitted display suppresses the reasoning **text**, not the block. The block must still exist, because its signature has to be replayed unmodified on later turns — so the stream shows `content_block_start`, one `signature_delta`, `content_block_stop`, and no `thinking_delta`. A client that assumes every opened block yields deltas will mishandle this shape. **Currency note:** on some current models thinking cannot be disabled at all, only hidden this way.",
w:"B — the block is present in the stream; only its text is withheld. C — no `thinking_delta` events are emitted under omitted display. D — what arrives before the block closes is the signature, not the reasoning text."},

{id:1112,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A streaming client sets a 30-second HTTP timeout. Short answers work; long ones that begin with a reasoning phase fail with a client-side timeout while the connection is still healthy. What is the correct timeout design?",
o:["A. Remove all timeouts, since SSE connections manage their own liveness","B. Keep the 30-second total timeout and retry on expiry; long generations are not supported over SSE","C. Set the timeout to the worst-case total generation time and treat any earlier silence as success","D. Use an idle timeout that resets on every received event, `ping` included, rather than a timeout on total request duration — paired with a generous absolute ceiling"],a:3,
e:"A total-duration timeout punishes long, healthy responses; the meaningful signal is **silence**, not elapsed time. Reset the clock on every event, which is precisely why the API interleaves `ping` keep-alives — they prove liveness during a reasoning phase that emits no content. Keep a generous absolute ceiling so a genuinely wedged connection still fails, and set TCP socket keep-alive on direct integrations to blunt idle-connection teardown.",
w:"A — an unbounded read hangs forever behind a silently dropped connection. B — long generations are exactly what streaming is for; the timeout is misconfigured, not the transport. C — treating silence as success inverts the diagnosis; an idle stream is the condition that must be detected."},

{id:1113,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A regulated chat product must redact account numbers before any text reaches the browser. The team runs its regex on each `text_delta` as it arrives. In testing, some account numbers get through. What is the underlying problem?",
o:["A. Regexes cannot be applied to streamed content; redaction must be delegated to the model through the system prompt","B. Deltas split at arbitrary character boundaries, so a pattern can straddle two events and match neither — the filter needs a sliding buffer that holds back the tail until a safe boundary","C. `text_delta` events are delivered out of order, so the filter sees fragments in the wrong sequence","D. The filter must run on `message_stop`, which carries the assembled text for exactly this purpose"],a:1,
e:"Streamed text is a byte stream, not a sequence of records: `4111 1111` may arrive as `411` then `1 1111`, and neither fragment matches. Any matcher whose patterns can exceed one delta must **buffer** — emit only up to the last position where no partial match is pending, and flush at `content_block_stop`. Principle: **the model is not a security boundary**, so redaction stays in deterministic code; streaming only means that code must be incremental-safe.",
w:"A — deterministic redaction in code is right; a prompt instruction is advisory and cannot be relied on for a control. C — deltas for a given `index` arrive in order; fragmentation, not reordering, is the issue. D — `message_stop` carries only its `type`, and deferring all filtering to the end forfeits progressive display."},

{id:1114,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A cost dashboard built on streamed calls reports input tokens as zero for every request, while output tokens look right. The team reads `usage` from each `message_delta` and keeps the last one. What is going wrong?",
o:["A. Streamed requests are billed on output tokens only, so zero is correct","B. `usage` is populated only when prompt caching is disabled","C. The input-side counters are published on `message_start`, so a collector that inspects only the tail of the stream can miss them","D. Input tokens must be obtained from a separate `/v1/messages/count_tokens` call, which streaming does not perform"],a:2,
e:"The stream reports usage in two places for a structural reason: input accounting is settled when the request is accepted and rides on **`message_start`**, while output accounting accrues and is reported cumulatively on **`message_delta`**. A collector that latches only the final `message_delta` is reading the output-side view. Capture `message_start.usage` for input and cache counters, and the last `message_delta` for cumulative output plus `stop_reason`.",
w:"A — input tokens are billed on every request, streamed or not. B — caching adds counters to `usage`; it does not suppress them. D — `count_tokens` is a free pre-flight estimate with its own rate limit, not a way to account for a call you have already made."},

{id:1115,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A service that previously used non-streaming calls switches to streaming and begins refusing traffic at the same request volume. Threads sit in read loops and the outbound connection pool is saturated. What is the structural cause?",
o:["A. Streaming holds one connection open for the whole generation, so concurrency is now bounded by connections and worker slots rather than by request rate — the fix is non-blocking I/O plus an explicit concurrency budget","B. Streamed requests consume four times the requests-per-minute quota, so the rate limit is being hit","C. SSE requires a dedicated connection per content block, multiplying connection use by block count","D. Throughput fell because streamed responses are generated more slowly than buffered ones"],a:0,
e:"A non-streaming call also occupies a connection for its duration, but the worker parks on a single read; streaming parks a worker in a loop for tens of seconds while events trickle in. Same wall-clock, very different resource shape. The remedies are architectural: async I/O so one thread multiplexes many streams, a bounded semaphore on concurrent streams, and a connection pool sized to peak **concurrent streams** rather than to requests per second.",
w:"B — streaming does not multiply request count; one request remains one request. C — a single HTTP connection carries the entire stream whatever its block count. D — generation speed is unchanged; only delivery is incremental."},

{id:1116,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"How is each event framed on the wire in a Claude streaming response?",
o:["A. As newline-delimited JSON with no event names","B. As one JSON array streamed incrementally, which the client parses once it is complete","C. As WebSocket frames, one message per delta","D. As a server-sent event with a named event line — for example `event: message_stop` — followed by a `data:` line whose JSON repeats the same value in its `type` field"],a:3,
e:"The transport is plain **SSE over the ordinary `POST /v1/messages`**: each event carries an SSE event name and a `data:` payload whose JSON includes a matching `type`. That deliberate redundancy lets a client dispatch on either. A hand-rolled parser must split on the blank-line event delimiter, tolerate `ping` and `error` at any position, and ignore names it does not recognise, since new event types may be added.",
w:"A — events do carry names; a line-per-event JSONL rendering is a client-side convenience, not the wire format. B — events are framed individually and nothing waits for a closing bracket. C — no WebSocket is involved; it is a normal HTTP response body."},

{id:1117,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team adds structured logging to its streaming handler, emitting one INFO record per received event. Log volume rises by two orders of magnitude and p99 latency worsens. What is the proportionate approach?",
o:["A. Keep per-event logging but at DEBUG level, so production log filtering removes the cost","B. Log only `error` events; everything else is noise","C. Log per-request aggregates — event counts by type, time to first delta, final `stop_reason`, `usage`, and the `request-id` — keeping full event traces behind a sampled or on-demand switch","D. Write every event asynchronously, which removes the latency cost while keeping full fidelity"],a:2,
e:"One response can produce hundreds of deltas, so per-event logging turns an observability aid into a load generator — the formatting work alone competes with the render path. Log at the granularity you would query: one record per request carrying what lets you triage, namely `request-id` for support tickets, `stop_reason` for truncation and refusal rates, `usage` for cost, and time-to-first-delta plus event counts for latency.",
w:"A — DEBUG records are usually still constructed and formatted before being discarded, and the volume returns the moment anyone enables them. B — errors alone reveal nothing about truncation, refusals, cost or latency. D — asynchronous writes relocate the cost rather than removing it, and the storage volume is unchanged."},

{id:1118,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team must test the handler that drives its chat UI from a stream: per-`index` accumulation, tool-argument assembly, `ping` tolerance, mid-stream `error` handling, and truncation on `max_tokens`. What is the soundest strategy?",
o:["A. Run the tests against the live API with a low `max_tokens`, since only real traffic produces realistic event sequences","B. Replay recorded and hand-authored SSE event sequences into the handler as fixtures, covering multiple blocks, interleaved `ping`, a mid-stream `error`, and each `stop_reason` the UI must handle","C. Assert only on the final accumulated text, since intermediate state is an implementation detail","D. Mock the SDK's `finalMessage()` helper so the handler receives a completed `Message`"],a:1,
e:"The handler is effectively a pure function from an event sequence to UI state, which makes fixtures the natural test vehicle. Live calls are non-deterministic, slow, cost money, and cannot be made to emit an `overloaded_error` on demand. Author the awkward sequences by hand: two text blocks either side of a tool block, a `ping` between deltas, an `error` at 60%, and `stop_reason` values of `max_tokens` and `refusal`.",
w:"A — live responses cannot be forced to produce mid-stream errors or specific stop reasons, and their non-determinism makes assertions flaky. C — the bugs live in intermediate state: mis-keyed indices, prematurely parsed tool input, ignored keep-alives. D — mocking the accumulator bypasses the very code under test."},

{id:1119,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A streamed answer renders fully in the UI, and then `message_delta` reports `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`. Product wants correct behaviour rather than a silent truncation. What should the client do?",
o:["A. Treat `stop_reason` as part of the response contract: on `max_tokens`, mark the displayed text as incomplete and offer a continuation rather than presenting it as a finished answer","B. Suppress the rendered text entirely, since a truncated answer is invalid","C. Retry the identical request; `max_tokens` truncation is non-deterministic and usually clears","D. Ignore it — in a stream, `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` only means the final delta was clipped"],a:0,
e:"Streaming shows text before you know why generation stopped, which makes the terminal `stop_reason` a **UI state transition**, not a log field. On `max_tokens` the text is genuine but cut short: label it and offer continuation or a larger cap. The general shape is a switch at `message_delta` over `end_turn`, `max_tokens`, `stop_sequence`, `tool_use`, `pause_turn`, `refusal` and `model_context_window_exceeded`, with a default branch, since the enum may grow.",
w:"B — the generated text is valid output and the user has already read it; discarding it wastes paid work. C — the same prompt against the same cap truncates again; the fix is a larger budget or a smaller task. D — it means the whole generation hit the output cap, not that one delta was clipped."},

{id:1120,d:"D1",s:"Streaming",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Two implementations of one feature are benchmarked. The streaming version shows first text at 600 ms and finishes at 9.2 s; the non-streaming version returns nothing until 9.1 s. Stakeholders ask why streaming is \"15 times faster\". How should the engineer characterise it?",
o:["A. Streaming genuinely reduces generation time by overlapping token production with delivery","B. The comparison is invalid because streamed and non-streamed requests use different models","C. Streaming is faster because SSE compresses the response body","D. Streaming does not reduce total time to a complete answer; it reduces time to first token, which is a perceived-latency win — and it disappears entirely if the consumer needs the whole answer before acting"],a:3,
e:"Generation cost is the same either way; streaming changes **when bytes become visible**. That is a large, real gain for a reading human and no gain at all for a parser that needs a complete JSON document or for a database writer. Choose by consumer: progressive human display → stream; whole-artefact consumer with no deadline → batch for the ~50% discount; whole-artefact consumer with a deadline → one buffered call.",
w:"A — token production is not accelerated; only delivery is incremental. B — nothing suggests a model difference, and the near-identical completion times are precisely the point. C — SSE applies no compression; event framing makes the payload marginally larger."},

{id:1121,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What endpoint and method create a Message Batch?",
o:["A. `POST /v1/messages` with `\"batch\": true` in the body","B. `POST /v1/messages/batches` with a `requests` array","C. `PUT /v1/batches` with a JSONL body","D. `POST /v1/messages/batches/submit` with a multipart upload"],a:1,
e:"A batch is created by posting a **`requests`** array to **`/v1/messages/batches`**, where each element is `{custom_id, params}` and `params` holds an otherwise-ordinary Messages body. The rest of the surface follows: `GET /v1/messages/batches/{id}` to poll, `.../results` for the JSONL output, `POST .../cancel`, `DELETE /v1/messages/batches/{id}`, and `GET /v1/messages/batches` to list with pagination.",
w:"A — there is no batch flag on the Messages endpoint; batching is a separate resource with its own lifecycle. C — creation is a JSON POST, and JSONL is the format of the results, not of the input. D — no multipart upload step exists."},

{id:1122,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A pipeline submits 100,000 classification requests, polls until `processing_status` is `ended`, then writes `result.message.content[0].text` for every line of the results file. It crashes partway through, and after a re-run about 3% of source records still have no classification. What is the flaw?",
o:["A. The results file is truncated because it must be paginated with `after_id`","B. Results become readable only 29 days after creation, so an early read is incomplete","C. `ended` means processing has finished, not that every request succeeded: `errored`, `canceled` and `expired` results carry no `message`, so each line must be branched on `result.type`","D. `processing_status` should be polled until `succeeded`, the terminal state the code is missing"],a:2,
e:"Batch is a **partial-failure system by design** — the failure of one request does not affect the others. `ended` means nothing is still processing. The batch's `request_counts` splits the outcome into `processing`, `succeeded`, `errored`, `canceled` and `expired`, and every results line carries a `result.type` from the same vocabulary. Only `succeeded` nests a `message`; dereferencing `result.message` on the others throws, which is exactly the crash observed.",
w:"A — the results file streams as JSONL from `results_url`; the list endpoint paginates, the results file does not. B — results are available as soon as processing ends and stay downloadable for 29 days from creation. D — `succeeded` is a per-request result type; the batch-level `processing_status` never takes that value."},

{id:1123,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which set of fields does a Message Batch's `request_counts` object report?",
o:["A. `processing`, `succeeded`, `errored`, `canceled`, `expired`","B. `pending`, `complete`, `failed`, `retried`","C. `queued`, `running`, `done`, `error`","D. `total`, `succeeded`, `failed`"],a:0,
e:"**`request_counts`** is the batch-level roll-up and uses the same vocabulary as each per-request `result.type`, plus `processing` for requests still in flight. It is the cheapest diagnostic available: a batch that ended with a large `errored` count usually means a systematic problem with the request shape, whereas a large `expired` count points at throughput and the 24-hour window. The two demand opposite remedies.",
w:"B — this is not the API's vocabulary, and the Batch API does not retry your requests for you. C — no `queued` or `running` fields exist; requests still in flight are counted under `processing`. D — a bare total-and-failed split would hide the distinction between `errored` and `expired`, which is the distinction that determines what you do next."},

{id:1124,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A batch of 40,000 requests ends with 39,100 `succeeded` and 900 `errored`. Inspecting the errored lines shows a mix: some carry `invalid_request_error`, the rest carry other error types. What is the correct remediation?",
o:["A. Resubmit the entire batch, because partial resubmission breaks `custom_id` uniqueness","B. Resubmit only the errored subset unchanged, since the Batch API retries transient failures internally","C. Treat all 900 as permanent failures, because a batch result cannot be retried","D. Resubmit only the failed subset — fixing the request bodies behind `invalid_request_error` first, and re-sending the other error types as they are"],a:3,
e:"The documented handlers draw exactly this line: an `errored` result with `invalid_request_error` means the **request body must be fixed before re-sending**, while other error types can be retried directly. Cost favours the subset too, since you are not billed for `errored`, `canceled` or `expired` requests. Select the failures by `custom_id` and keep the same ids, so downstream joins continue to work.",
w:"A — re-running 39,100 successful requests pays for them twice for no benefit, and `custom_id` need only be unique within a batch. B — a validation error fails identically however often it is resubmitted; the batch does not silently repair malformed bodies. C — batch results are ordinary request outcomes and the failed subset is straightforwardly resubmittable."},

{id:1125,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An engineer submits a 60,000-request batch late on Friday. It is accepted with `processing_status: \"in_progress\"`. Hours later it ends with 60,000 `errored` results, all `invalid_request_error` on the same malformed field. Why did creation succeed, and what practice prevents this?",
o:["A. Creation validates only `custom_id`; a paid pre-validation endpoint exists for `params`","B. Validation of each request's `params` is performed asynchronously and surfaces only once processing has ended, so the safeguard is to dry-run one request of that shape against the Messages API first","C. Validation is skipped whenever a batch exceeds 50,000 requests","D. `params` are validated at creation, so the error must have come from a mid-batch model change"],a:1,
e:"The create call checks the batch envelope, not the contents of every `params` object: **validation of `params` is performed asynchronously, and validation errors are returned when processing of the entire batch has ended.** The consequence is a feedback loop measured in hours instead of milliseconds. The documented best practice is to verify the request shape with the synchronous Messages API first — one real call costs cents and collapses the loop to seconds.",
w:"A — there is no separate paid pre-validation endpoint; `/v1/messages/count_tokens` is free but does not validate a full request. C — batch size does not change validation behaviour; validation is asynchronous at any size. D — `params` are not validated at creation, which is precisely why acceptance was never a signal of correctness."},

{id:1126,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which Messages API parameters are rejected inside a Message Batch request?",
o:["A. `tools`, `system`, and `thinking`","B. `stop_sequences` and `metadata`","C. `stream: true`, `speed` (fast mode), and `max_tokens: 0`","D. `cache_control` and image content blocks"],a:2,
e:"Almost everything batches — vision, tool use including server tools, system prompts, multi-turn history, thinking, most betas — and different request types can be mixed in one batch. The three exclusions follow from what a batch **is**: results arrive as one file so there is nothing to stream; fast mode tunes synchronous latency, which is meaningless asynchronously; and `max_tokens: 0` cache pre-warming is pointless because the ephemeral entry would expire before any follow-up ran. Each batched request needs `max_tokens` of at least 1.",
w:"A — tools, system prompts and thinking are all supported and can vary per request. B — both are ordinary supported parameters. D — prompt caching is explicitly supported and recommended in batches with the 1-hour TTL, and images batch normally."},

{id:1127,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Streaming and the Batch API are often described as opposite tools. Which statement pairs them correctly?",
o:["A. Both reduce cost: streaming by compressing output, batching by discounting it","B. Both reduce latency: streaming for interactive work, batching for bulk work","C. Streaming reduces cost and batching reduces latency","D. Streaming optimises perceived latency at standard pricing; batching optimises cost and throughput by giving up latency altogether, which is why `stream: true` is rejected inside a batch"],a:3,
e:"They sit at opposite ends of one trade-off. Streaming spends nothing extra and buys **time to first token** for a human watching output appear. Batching spends up to 24 hours of wall-clock and buys roughly a **50% discount** plus higher throughput, delivering results as a single JSONL file — hence `stream: true` being a validation error inside a batch. The deciding question is whether anyone is waiting on the answer.",
w:"A — streaming applies neither compression nor any discount. B — batching increases latency by design; that is what the discount is paid for. C — this inverts both halves: streaming has no price effect and batching has no latency benefit."},

{id:1128,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A batch of 30,000 requests shares a 180,000-token reference document behind a `cache_control` breakpoint. The run takes about nine hours. `cache_read_input_tokens` is high early and near zero later, and spend is well above the model. What change most improves it?",
o:["A. Use the 1-hour cache TTL, because the default 5-minute ephemeral entry expires during a batch that runs for hours","B. Add a second breakpoint after the user question so each request also caches its own tail","C. Split into 30,000 single-request batches so each gets a fresh cache entry","D. Remove `cache_control`; caching and the batch discount cannot be combined"],a:0,
e:"The default ephemeral TTL is **five minutes, refreshed on each read** — fine for steady synchronous traffic, fragile across a nine-hour asynchronous run where any lull lets the entry lapse and the next request pays the write again. The docs recommend the **1-hour cache duration** for batches with shared context for exactly this reason. The discounts stack: the batch discount and the cache read multiplier apply together. Expect best-effort hit rates, commonly quoted as 30–98%.",
w:"B — the per-request tail is unique, so caching it can never be read again and only adds write cost. C — thousands of one-request batches multiply overhead, forfeit the shared prefix entirely, and burn the Batches requests-per-minute limit. D — the two features are explicitly designed to stack."},

{id:1129,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"20,000 batch requests each prepend the same 100,000-token document, then ask a short unique question. Team A caches the document with the 1-hour TTL; Team B does not cache. Both use the Batch API. Which best describes the economics?",
o:["A. Roughly equal, because the batch discount already applies to the document tokens and caching adds nothing","B. Team B is cheaper: the 2× write premium for a 1-hour entry outweighs the savings at this volume","C. Team A is far cheaper: one 2× write amortises across up to 20,000 reads billed at 0.1× base input, and the batch discount applies on top","D. Team A is cheaper only if all 20,000 requests are submitted inside the same 60-second window"],a:2,
e:"Caching converts a per-request cost into a one-off. Write once at **2× base input** for the 1-hour TTL, then read at **0.1×** — removing roughly nine-tenths of the document's contribution on every later request — with the ~50% batch discount multiplying through. Break-even arrives after a handful of reads, so at 20,000 it is not close. Honest caveat: batch cache hits are best-effort because requests run concurrently and in any order, so model the saving as a range.",
w:"A — the discount halves the price of those tokens; caching removes most of what is left. B — the write premium is paid once, not per request, and is recovered within a few reads. D — the 1-hour TTL is what removes the need for a tight submission window, and each read refreshes the entry."},

{id:1130,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A nightly report must be in inboxes by 06:00. The team submits a 12,000-request batch at 05:15, reasoning that most batches finish in under an hour. It works for weeks, then misses twice in one month. What is the design error?",
o:["A. The batch should have carried a priority flag to guarantee the window","B. The 24-hour window is a ceiling, not an estimate: completion depends on batch size, current demand and your own request volume, and requests can even expire — so a deadline-bound job must not depend on it","C. 12,000 requests exceeds the per-batch limit, so the batch was silently split and queued behind other work","D. Batches are processed in platform-wide submission order, so a late submission is always served late"],a:1,
e:"The guarantee is a negative one: you can access results when all messages have completed **or after 24 hours, whichever comes first**, and batches expire if processing does not complete within 24 hours. Most finish in under an hour, but that is an observation rather than an SLA, and processing may be slowed by demand or your own volume. For a hard deadline, submit far earlier with a synchronous fallback path — or do not batch this work.",
w:"A — the Batches API offers no priority or expedite option. C — 12,000 is well inside the 100,000-request limit, and batches are not silently split. D — requests are processed concurrently and independently, not through a global first-in-first-out queue."},

{id:1131,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Six weeks after a batch ran, an auditor asks for its raw results. The batch is still visible in the Console. What should the team expect?",
o:["A. Results remain downloadable indefinitely once a batch has ended","B. Results are retained for 24 hours after the batch ends and are certainly gone","C. Results can be regenerated by re-reading `results_url`, which never expires","D. Results are available for 29 days from the batch's `created_at`, after which the batch is still viewable but its results are no longer downloadable — so any retained copy must come from the team's own storage"],a:3,
e:"Retention is **29 days measured from `created_at`**, not from when processing ended. Past that the batch record persists while the results do not, which makes durable archival the caller's job: stream the JSONL as soon as processing ends and write it somewhere you control. If you need the data gone sooner, `DELETE /v1/messages/batches/{batch_id}` removes it on demand.",
w:"A — retention is bounded at 29 days. B — 24 hours is the processing window before requests expire, not the results retention period. C — `results_url` stops serving the file once retention lapses, and nothing is regenerated."},

{id:1132,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A worker submits 200 small batches per hour and polls each batch's retrieve endpoint every 200 ms until it ends. The service starts taking 429s on the Batches endpoints, including on batch creation. What is the fix?",
o:["A. Poll on an interval of tens of seconds to minutes with backoff: the Batches API has its own requests-per-minute limit shared across its endpoints, and polling is competing with creation for it","B. Move polling to the results endpoint, which is not rate limited","C. Raise `max_tokens` so batches finish sooner and need fewer polls","D. Poll from several API keys in the same organisation to spread the load"],a:0,
e:"The Batches API has its **own rate limits**: a requests-per-minute limit across its HTTP endpoints, plus a cap on how many batch requests may sit in the processing queue. Sub-second polling of a job whose horizon is minutes to hours spends the entire quota asking questions, and starves creation calls. The documented polling loop sleeps on the order of a minute between retrievals.",
w:"B — every Batches endpoint counts toward the same requests-per-minute limit. C — `max_tokens` is an output cap that does not accelerate queueing, and a larger cap makes generations longer. D — rate limits are enforced per organisation, so extra keys add no capacity."},

{id:1133,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which statement about Message Batches API rate limits is correct?",
o:["A. Batch usage draws down the same input- and output-tokens-per-minute limits as the Messages API","B. The Batches API has its own limits — a requests-per-minute limit on its endpoints and a cap on the number of batch requests awaiting processing — and its usage does not affect Messages API rate limits","C. Batches are exempt from rate limiting, which is why they are cheaper","D. Each batch counts as a single request against the Messages API requests-per-minute limit"],a:1,
e:"Batch limits form a separate regime, shared across models, with two dimensions: HTTP **requests per minute** to the Batches endpoints, and how many individual **batch requests may be awaiting processing** at once. One submission of thousands of requests consumes the second budget heavily. Usefully, the docs state that Batches API usage does not affect Messages API rate limits — so a large backfill need not starve interactive traffic.",
w:"A — the two regimes are separate, and that separation is what makes bulk backfills safe alongside live traffic. C — batches are rate limited, just differently; the discount comes from deferred, schedulable execution. D — the queue limit counts individual batch requests, not whole batches."},

{id:1134,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An operator cancels a running 50,000-request batch after spotting a bad prompt. The immediate response shows `processing_status: \"canceling\"` with `request_counts.canceled` at 0. A colleague concludes the cancel failed. What actually happens?",
o:["A. Cancellation is rejected once any request has been processed, so the batch runs to completion","B. `canceling` is terminal, and the results file is discarded along with any work already done","C. Cancellation is asynchronous: the status moves from `canceling` to `ended`, results retain whatever succeeded before cancellation, and requests never sent to the model are recorded as `canceled` and not billed","D. `request_counts.canceled` stays at 0 because canceled requests are removed from the batch entirely"],a:2,
e:"Cancel is a request, not a switch: **immediately after cancellation `processing_status` is `canceling`**, and you poll exactly as you would for completion until it settles at **`ended`**, possibly holding partial results for requests processed before the cancel took effect. Unstarted requests land as `canceled` and are not billed, alongside `errored` and `expired`. Note also that a submitted batch cannot be edited — cancel and resubmit is the only path.",
w:"A — a processing batch can be canceled at any point; cancellation simply is not instantaneous. B — `canceling` is transient, and already-succeeded results are preserved. D — canceled requests are counted under `request_counts.canceled` once cancellation settles; the zero reflects timing, not removal."},

{id:1135,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Twenty minutes into a 100,000-request batch, a team realises 200 requests reference the wrong document. What is the correct course of action, and what does it cost?",
o:["A. `PATCH` the batch to replace those 200 requests; a batch is mutable while `in_progress`","B. Submit a second batch with corrected versions of the 200; matching `custom_id` values cause them to supersede the originals","C. Let the batch finish and delete the 200 bad results, which are not billed because they were superseded","D. A submitted batch cannot be modified, so either accept the 200 wrong results and resubmit those alone, or cancel the whole batch and resubmit — noting that cancellation is not immediate and anything already processed is billed"],a:3,
e:"Immutability is the rule: **once submitted, a batch cannot be modified**; if you need changes you cancel the current batch and submit a new one, and cancellation may not take effect immediately. With 200 of 100,000 affected, the cheap move is almost always to let the batch finish and resubmit the corrected 200 as their own batch — cancelling throws away 99,800 results you have already partly paid for.",
w:"A — no update endpoint exists for a batch. B — `custom_id` is a correlation handle scoped to its own batch and supersedes nothing across batches. C — requests that ran successfully are billed whether or not you use their output."},

{id:1136,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A generator builds a batch by hashing each document's first 200 characters into its `custom_id`. Several near-identical templates collide, so a few ids repeat. Beyond breaking the uniqueness requirement, what practical harm follows?",
o:["A. Results cannot be correlated back to their source records, because `custom_id` is the only join key in the results file and duplicates make the mapping ambiguous","B. The colliding requests are deduplicated and billed once, silently dropping work","C. `custom_id` collisions cause the batch to be split across multiple results files","D. Nothing practical; ordering within the results file resolves the ambiguity"],a:0,
e:"Results come back as JSONL in **arbitrary order**, so `custom_id` is the entire correlation mechanism — the docs state that results may not match input order and that you should always match on `custom_id`. Duplicates therefore destroy the join, and no other field identifies the source record. Each id must be unique within its batch and match `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$`. Derive ids from the primary key of the source record, never from a hash of its content.",
w:"B — the API does not deduplicate batch requests; it requires unique ids instead. C — a batch produces one results file whatever its ids. D — results are explicitly not returned in input order, so position cannot serve as a fallback."},

{id:1137,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What are the format constraints on a batch request's `custom_id`?",
o:["A. Any string up to 1,024 characters, including spaces and punctuation","B. A client-generated UUID; other formats are rejected","C. 1 to 64 characters of alphanumerics, hyphens and underscores — matching `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$` — and unique within the batch","D. Optional; the API assigns sequential ids when it is omitted"],a:2,
e:"**`custom_id` is required on every request in a batch** and constrained to `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$`, the same character class as a tool name. The practical consequence bites in real pipelines: a raw file path, an email address or a URL cannot be used directly and must be encoded or mapped. Because results are unordered, this short token is the only link between an output line and the record that produced it.",
w:"A — the limit is 64 characters, and the character class excludes spaces and most punctuation. B — a UUID happens to fit the pattern but is not required; any conforming string works. D — omitting `custom_id` is invalid, and nothing is auto-assigned."},

{id:1138,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A pipeline reads the results JSONL and zips the lines positionally against the input array it submitted. Spot checks pass on a 10-request test; at 50,000 requests, summaries are attached to the wrong documents. What went wrong?",
o:["A. The results file interleaves lines from concurrent workers, so it must be sorted by `created_at` before use","B. Batch results can be returned in any order and need not match submission order; every line must be joined on its `custom_id`","C. The results file is paginated, and later pages restart their line numbering","D. The pipeline must request `preserve_order: true` at batch creation"],a:1,
e:"The docs warn directly that **batch results may not match input order**, and even the two-request example in the guide returns the second request's result first. Concurrency makes reordering the norm at scale, while a tiny test can pass by luck — a classic scale-dependent bug. Each line is `{custom_id, result}`: build a map from `custom_id` to your record and join on it, never on position.",
w:"A — there is no ordering key that would recover submission order by sorting. C — the results file is one JSONL stream, not a paginated collection. D — no ordering option exists; `custom_id` is the intended mechanism."},

{id:1139,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team submits 60,000 requests, each embedding a base64 page image, and the create call fails with `413 request_too_large`. They point out that 60,000 is well under the documented request-count limit. What is happening, and what is the better design?",
o:["A. Images are not supported in batches, so the payload is rejected on size grounds","B. The limit drops to 60,000 requests when any request contains an image; split the batch in half","C. Base64 images must be compressed before inclusion, which brings the payload under the limit","D. A batch is capped at 100,000 requests *or* 256 MB, whichever is reached first; split into smaller batches and reference images by Files API `file_id` rather than inlining base64 bytes"],a:3,
e:"The two limits are **alternatives, not a conjunction** — whichever binds first wins. Base64 inflates bytes by roughly a third, so image-heavy batches hit the 256 MB wall long before the request count. The Messages API caps one request at 32 MB and the Batch API caps a submission at 256 MB, returning `413 request_too_large`. Upload once through the Files API and send `file` source blocks carrying a `file_id`, which also avoids re-sending identical bytes.",
w:"A — vision requests batch normally; size rather than modality is the problem. B — no image-specific request-count limit exists; total payload bytes is the binding constraint. C — the API expects base64 image data, not a separately compressed blob, and no such content encoding is accepted."},

{id:1140,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"The results file for a 100,000-request batch is several gigabytes. A worker downloads it into a string and parses it, and is killed by the container's memory limit. What does the documented pattern look like?",
o:["A. Stream the results back line by line — every SDK exposes a results iterator for this — and handle each `{custom_id, result}` record as it arrives","B. Request the results in pages of 1,000 using `limit` and `after_id`","C. Re-run the batch with fewer requests, since results files above 1 GB are unsupported","D. Fetch `results_url` with a `Range` header to slice the file into fixed byte windows"],a:0,
e:"The docs recommend streaming results back rather than downloading them all at once, and each SDK exposes an iterator that yields one parsed result at a time. **JSONL is chosen precisely because it is line-delimited**: memory stays flat regardless of batch size, and each record can be routed by `custom_id` and dispatched on `result.type` the moment it appears. Persist as you go so a crash does not restart the download.",
w:"B — pagination applies to the list-batches endpoint, not to the results file. C — there is no results-file size ceiling to work around; the consumption pattern is what is wrong. D — arbitrary byte ranges cut JSON objects mid-line, and the line-oriented iterator already solves this."},

{id:1141,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What does a batch results file look like?",
o:["A. A single JSON object mapping each `custom_id` to its message","B. `.jsonl` — one JSON object per line, each carrying a `custom_id` and a `result` whose `type` is `succeeded`, `errored`, `canceled` or `expired`","C. A CSV with columns for `custom_id`, text and token counts","D. A tar archive holding one JSON file per request"],a:1,
e:"**JSONL keeps the file streamable**: each line stands alone, so a consumer parses and dispatches records one at a time whatever the batch's size. A `succeeded` line nests the full `Message` under `result.message`, including `content`, `stop_reason` and `usage`; an errored line carries the standard error shape under `result.error`. There is no envelope and no ordering guarantee, which is why `custom_id` does the joining.",
w:"A — a single object would have to be parsed whole, defeating streamed consumption. C — the payload is a nested `Message` object, which does not flatten into fixed columns. D — no archive format is involved; `results_url` serves one JSONL stream."},

{id:1142,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which statement about feature support inside a Message Batch is correct?",
o:["A. Only single-turn text requests may be batched; anything richer must use the Messages API","B. All requests in one batch must share the same model and the same parameters","C. Almost any Messages request can be batched — vision, tool use including server tools, system prompts, multi-turn history, thinking, most betas — and different request types can be mixed freely in one batch","D. Client tool use is supported but server tools such as web search are not, because they need an open connection"],a:2,
e:"Each request in a batch is **processed independently** against the ordinary Messages pipeline, so the feature surface is nearly identical and one batch can mix models, modalities and parameters at will. Server tools run too: the batch worker executes the same server-side agentic loop, and because it holds no open connection it runs **more iterations per turn** than a synchronous request before returning `stop_reason: \"pause_turn\"`.",
w:"A — multi-turn, vision and tool-using requests all batch normally. B — models and parameters may differ per request, since each is independent. D — server tools including web search do work in batches; the worker throttles web search per organisation and retries internally."},

{id:1143,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A finance team sets a workspace spend limit and finds that a large batch overshot it by a small margin, whereas synchronous requests stop at the limit reliably. What explains the difference?",
o:["A. Batch spend is billed to the organisation rather than the workspace, so workspace limits do not apply","B. The batch discount is applied after the limit check, so the recorded overshoot is an accounting artefact","C. Spend limits apply only to input tokens, and the overshoot is entirely output","D. Because of high throughput and concurrent processing, batches may go slightly over a workspace's configured spend limit — the limit is a brake, not a transactional gate for asynchronous work"],a:3,
e:"The docs say it plainly: because of high throughput and concurrent processing, **batches may go slightly over your Workspace's configured spend limit**. Thousands of requests are already in flight when the threshold is crossed, and there is no way to unwind them. Size batches with that in mind. Note also which code you get where: a spend limit you configured returns **400**, whereas your usage tier's monthly cap returns **429** with no `retry-after`.",
w:"A — batches are scoped to the Workspace they were created in and count against it. B — the discount changes the amount, not the ordering of the check; the overshoot is real spend. C — spend limits track cost across all token categories, input and output alike."},

{id:1144,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team dumps 450,000 requests as five 90,000-request batches at once, every night. Most complete, but a growing share come back `expired`. What is the most productive response?",
o:["A. Smooth submission — fewer requests in flight at a time, staggered over the night — because the Batches API caps how many batch requests may await processing, and heavy volume can slow processing until requests hit the 24-hour expiry","B. Resubmit the expired requests immediately in a sixth batch each night, since expired requests are not billed","C. Raise `max_tokens` on the expired requests so they are scheduled with higher priority","D. Accept the loss: expiry is random and unavoidable at this volume"],a:0,
e:"**`expired` means the 24-hour window closed before the request reached the model.** The docs tie this to throughput: processing may be slowed based on current demand and your request volume, and in that case you may see more requests expiring after 24 hours. The available levers are volume shaping — smaller batches, staggered submission, staying well inside the processing-queue cap — plus retrying the expired subset, for which the expired attempts themselves cost nothing.",
w:"B — retrying is necessary but not sufficient; stacking a sixth batch onto the same congested pipeline lengthens the queue. C — `max_tokens` confers no priority, and a larger cap means longer generations. D — expiry correlates with submission shape and is largely controllable."},

{id:1145,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A batch that uses the web search tool returns some results with `stop_reason: \"pause_turn\"`. The pipeline treats them as failures and discards them. What do those results actually mean?",
o:["A. The request was throttled and must be resubmitted from scratch","B. The batch worker does not support server tools, so the turn was abandoned","C. The server-side agentic loop handed control back before finishing; the turn can be continued by submitting the paused assistant content in a follow-up request, batch or synchronous","D. In a batch, `pause_turn` always means the 24-hour window expired mid-turn"],a:2,
e:"**`pause_turn` is a normal stop reason, not an error** — the server-side tool loop is pausing so you can continue it. Because a batch worker holds no open connection, it runs more iterations per turn than a synchronous call before pausing, so `pause_turn` is rarer here; when it appears, the remedy is the standard continuation pattern of replaying the paused assistant content. Discarding these results throws away work you have already paid for.",
w:"A — nothing was throttled, and the completed portion of the turn is reusable. B — server tools including web search do run in batches, on the same server-side loop. D — an expired request is reported as `result.type` of `expired`, with no message at all."},

{id:1146,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which statement about Message Batches billing is correct?",
o:["A. Every request in a batch is billed, since capacity was reserved for it","B. All usage is billed at 50% of standard prices, and requests ending as `errored`, `canceled` or `expired` are not billed at all","C. Batch input tokens are discounted, but output tokens are billed at standard rates","D. The discount applies only to batches of at least 1,000 requests"],a:1,
e:"The discount is **unconditional and applies to all usage** — input tokens, output tokens and special token categories alike — with no volume threshold. On top of that, only work the model actually performed is charged: `errored`, `canceled` and `expired` results cost nothing. Combined with prompt caching, whose read multiplier applies to the already-halved price, batch pricing is the single largest lever available on bulk workloads.",
w:"A — `errored`, `canceled` and `expired` requests are explicitly not billed. C — both input and output are discounted by the same 50%. D — no minimum batch size gates the discount; a two-request batch gets it."},

{id:1147,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A submit worker POSTs a 90,000-request batch, the call times out at the client, and the worker retries. Later, spend is roughly double the estimate and two batches appear in the list. What practice prevents this?",
o:["A. Include an `Idempotency-Key` header, which the Batches API honours on creation","B. Reuse the same `custom_id` values on the retry, so the API deduplicates the requests","C. Set `max_tokens: 0` on the retry so the duplicate costs nothing","D. Treat batch creation as non-idempotent: on an ambiguous failure, reconcile against the list endpoint before resubmitting, and persist the returned batch `id` as the record of submission"],a:3,
e:"A client-side timeout tells you nothing about whether the server accepted the request — and accepting a 90,000-request batch is an expensive thing to do twice. With no documented idempotency mechanism on creation, the safe pattern is **reconcile-then-retry**: list recent batches, look for one matching what you were submitting, and resubmit only if it is absent. Persist the batch `id` immediately so polling, auditing and cancellation all have a handle.",
w:"A — no idempotency key is documented for batch creation. B — `custom_id` is unique only within its own batch and provides no cross-batch deduplication. C — `max_tokens: 0` is rejected inside a batch, and shrinking output does not make a duplicate submission harmless."},

{id:1148,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A compliance rule requires batch inputs and outputs to be removed as soon as results have been archived internally. What does the API offer?",
o:["A. `DELETE /v1/messages/batches/{batch_id}` after processing; an in-progress batch must be canceled first","B. Nothing: batch data is retained for a fixed 29 days and cannot be removed earlier","C. A `retention_days` parameter set at batch creation","D. Deletion of the object behind `results_url`, which the caller owns"],a:0,
e:"Asynchronous processing requires server-side storage of both inputs and outputs until results are retrieved, and the default retention is **29 days from creation**. A batch can be deleted at any time after processing using **`DELETE /v1/messages/batches/{batch_id}`**; to remove one still running, cancel it first and delete once it has ended. Shortening retention is therefore an action you take, not a parameter you declare.",
w:"B — 29 days is the maximum retention, not a floor; explicit deletion is supported. C — no retention parameter exists on batch creation. D — `results_url` is served by Anthropic, not from storage the caller controls."},

{id:1149,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A CI job creates batches using a key scoped to the `staging` workspace. A separate reporting service, using a `production`-scoped key, gets 404s retrieving those batch ids and cannot list them. What is happening?",
o:["A. Batch ids are single-use and expire once retrieved","B. Batches are scoped to the Workspace they were created in and are visible only to requests running in that Workspace","C. Retrieval requires the same API key that created the batch, not merely the same workspace","D. Cross-workspace visibility requires an `anthropic-workspace-id` header on every retrieve call"],a:1,
e:"Batches are **isolated within their creating Workspace**: you may view all batches, and their results, that were created within the Workspace your request runs in — and none from any other. That is a deliberate privacy and data-separation boundary rather than a bug, so the fix is organisational: give the reporting service a key in the same Workspace, or create the batches where they need to be read. The `anthropic-workspace-id` response header tells you which Workspace a request resolved to.",
w:"A — batch ids are stable and retrievable repeatedly throughout the retention period. C — any key in the same Workspace can read the batch; the creating key's identity is not the boundary. D — the Workspace is derived from the credential, and no request header grants cross-workspace visibility."},

{id:1150,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"What should a batch monitor emit so an on-call engineer can triage a stalled or degraded batch without downloading results?",
o:["A. Only the terminal `processing_status`, since a batch is either running or ended","B. The full results file, tailed into a log sink","C. `processing_status`, the `request_counts` breakdown over time, age against `created_at` and `expires_at`, and alerts that distinguish a rising `errored` count from a rising `expired` count","D. The count of succeeded requests only, since the others are not billed"],a:2,
e:"The two failure modes demand **opposite responses**, so the monitor must separate them. A climbing `errored` count usually means a systematic request-shape problem: fix the body and resubmit that subset. A climbing `expired` count — or a `processing` count that barely moves as `expires_at` approaches — is a throughput problem, answered by shaping submission volume. Age against the 24-hour window is the leading indicator for the second, well before the batch ends.",
w:"A — a single terminal status says nothing about the 3% that failed, or why. B — the results file is large, arrives only at the end, and is an archive rather than a monitoring signal. D — succeeded-only reporting hides exactly the failures on-call needs, and unbilled failures still mean missing output."},

{id:1151,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A product offers users a \"deep analysis\" of an uploaded dataset that genuinely takes about 40 minutes of model work. Users start it from the web app. What role, if any, does the Batch API play?",
o:["A. None: anything a user initiates must use the synchronous Messages API","B. Submit to the Batch API but hold the HTTP request open until results are ready, showing a progress spinner","C. Use the Messages API with streaming, so the user watches 40 minutes of output arrive","D. Submit it as a batch job, return a job id immediately, poll server-side, and notify the user when results land — the interaction is already asynchronous, so the discount is free"],a:3,
e:"The deciding question is not who started the work but whether anyone **blocks** on the answer. A 40-minute job cannot be delivered inside a request either way, so the product needs a job record, a status endpoint and a notification regardless — and once that exists, batching costs nothing extra and halves the bill. Batch is the wrong choice only when a caller genuinely waits on the result within seconds.",
w:"A — user-initiated is not the same as latency-bound; long jobs need asynchronous product design whatever API they use. B — holding a request open for 40 minutes reproduces every idle-connection problem batching exists to avoid, and the 24-hour window gives no completion estimate to spin against. C — streaming does not shorten the 40 minutes and forfeits the discount to show output nobody is watching."},

{id:1152,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A migration must re-summarise 1,000,000 documents. Which submission plan fits the Batch API's constraints?",
o:["A. Shard into at least ten batches, since one batch caps at 100,000 requests or 256 MB whichever binds first, and stagger submission so the number of batch requests awaiting processing stays inside the queue limit","B. Submit one batch of 1,000,000 requests; the 100,000 figure is a per-minute limit, not a per-batch one","C. Submit ten batches of 100,000 simultaneously, since only per-batch limits exist","D. Submit 1,000,000 single-request batches to maximise parallelism"],a:0,
e:"Two ceilings shape the plan. **Per batch:** 100,000 requests or 256 MB, whichever is reached first — so at least ten batches, and more if payloads are large. **Across batches:** the Batches API caps how many individual batch requests may be waiting in the processing queue, so dumping everything at once queues work that then risks the 24-hour expiry. Stagger submission, watch `request_counts`, and cache the shared prefix with the 1-hour TTL.",
w:"B — 100,000 is a per-batch limit, not a rate. C — an organisation-wide processing-queue limit applies as well, and simultaneous submission is what drives `expired` results. D — a million single-request batches burn the requests-per-minute limit, add enormous overhead, and forfeit shared-prefix caching entirely."},

{id:1153,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Immediately after creating a batch, its `results_url` is `null` and `ended_at` is `null`. What does that indicate?",
o:["A. The create call partially failed and the batch should be resubmitted","B. Nothing is wrong: both fields populate only once `processing_status` reaches `ended` and results are ready to download","C. `results_url` must be requested separately with a beta header","D. Results are written incrementally, and `results_url` appears once the first request succeeds"],a:1,
e:"A freshly created batch reports `processing_status: \"in_progress\"`, `request_counts` with everything under `processing`, and `ended_at`, `cancel_initiated_at` and `results_url` all `null`. The results file is published when **processing ends** — the same condition your polling loop already waits on. Poll the retrieve endpoint on an interval of roughly a minute, then stream the file from `results_url`.",
w:"A — a null `results_url` on a new batch is the documented initial state, not a failure signal. C — no beta header is involved; the field populates on completion. D — results are published as one file at the end, not incrementally."},

{id:1154,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A summarisation batch of 80,000 requests ends with everything `succeeded`, but 12% of the summaries stop mid-sentence. `request_counts.errored` is 0. What is the likely cause and remedy?",
o:["A. Those requests were truncated by the 24-hour expiry and should be re-run as a smaller batch","B. Results were truncated during JSONL streaming; re-download the file","C. Those responses stopped with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` — a successful result that was merely capped — so inspect `stop_reason` per line and resubmit that subset with a larger `max_tokens`","D. The batch discount halves the output budget, so `max_tokens` must be doubled inside batches"],a:2,
e:"Batching changes scheduling and price, not generation semantics. A capped response is a **`succeeded`** result whose nested `Message` carries `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`, so a pipeline that inspects only `result.type` cannot see it. Validate the nested message too: switch on its `stop_reason` and treat `max_tokens` as a soft failure needing a larger cap or a smaller unit of work. Note that each batched request needs `max_tokens` of at least 1; `max_tokens: 0` is rejected.",
w:"A — expiry produces a `result.type` of `expired` with no message at all, not partial prose. B — a download problem would yield malformed JSON lines, not grammatically truncated summaries. D — the discount affects price only; the output budget is exactly what `max_tokens` specifies."},

{id:1155,d:"D1",s:"Batch API",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Finance compares a batch's invoice against an estimate built by calling `/v1/messages/count_tokens` on every request. Actual spend is materially lower. Which combination best explains it?",
o:["A. The estimate was wrong because `count_tokens` returns exact counts only for synchronous requests","B. Batch spend is invoiced a month in arrears, so the two figures cover different periods","C. `errored` and `expired` requests are billed at half the batch rate rather than not at all","D. Roughly a 50% batch discount across all usage, plus cache reads billed at a fraction of base input wherever a shared prefix was cached, plus no charge for requests that ended `errored`, `canceled` or `expired`"],a:3,
e:"Three effects compound and all point the same way: batch usage is charged at **50% of standard prices** across input, output and special tokens; a cached shared prefix is read at a **fraction of base input** on top of that; and requests the model never completed are **not billed**. A `count_tokens` estimate captures none of this — it returns only `input_tokens`, it is explicitly an estimate, and it does not exercise prompt caching.",
w:"A — `count_tokens` returns an estimate for any request, batched or not; the gap is not a batch-specific quirk of the endpoint. B — a timing difference cannot account for a materially different amount on the same batch. C — those requests are not billed at all, rather than billed at a reduced rate."}
);
